title The vessels get a listener
prompt Copal's first round -- the baton, and the seat's own first work: put amphora's witnesses on the standing roster and read what they say.
think Council rota row 0 of 5, lap N 3985 -- Aether, the row that hears. Its threshold page and the grain both carry the same strand, and it is the strand this seat was drawn for: a guard that cannot red guards nothing, and the roster's own header adds the second half, a guard that is never run guards nothing either. The aether lens sharpens it -- a witness must be proven able to make a sound before its silence means anything.
think Opened with fleet_round_open (already on the anointed order, 88721dde7f) and a cold roster pass, holding still while it ran. 114 guards, 838 seconds, 108 green, 4 gated, 2 red.
think Reds first, and the ledger answered where to stand: REDS %360 is OPEN and its remainder is exactly this lane. The seat prompt's claim checked out on metal -- git ls-files counts 10 tracked Rye modules and 3,861 lines under amphora/, and the standing roster named no guard over any of them.
think Ran all eight amphora witnesses BEFORE writing a roster row, because rostering an unrun guard is the same silence one layer up. All eight GREEN, 11 seconds together. Read amphora_pour_negative whole to check it was not a vacuum: three real plants, each refusal asserted by its own error name, and the standing vessel proven untouched after the forked pour. Four of the eight are negative twins, so the family already carried the teeth half of the law.
think Seated the eight at tier lap -- 11s against an 838s pass is lap money. Measured the fall rather than predicting it: heard 714 to 722, unheard 1,102 to 1,094, unheard_choirs unchanged at 37 because none of the eight names another guard.
think The ceiling was the one real judgment call. The reading already stood 14 UNDER the published 1,116 when the round opened -- slack earlier rosterings left. Publishing the measured 1,094 would tighten a fleet-wide lap-tier gate to zero headroom inside a round about vessels, and %360's own history records a fall cancelled by two witnesses landing upstream between measurement and send. So the ceiling falls by exactly the 8 these rows moved, 1,116 to 1,108, in both copies, and the 14 is named on the card for a hand that takes it deliberately.
think Two green guards named rather than taken -- kumara_contact and kumara_tilak read unheard beside these and drive kumara/ over tally/kumara.rye, which is the lane that owns Tally.
think The cold pass's second red is new information and got its own row. agent_jail reds here because the lap's own process reads NoNewPrivs 1 and Seccomp 2, so the sandbox already around it refuses bwrap's mount namespace -- bwrap --ro-bind / / true exits 1, unshare --mount true exits 1. fleet-loop.sh line 40 says Earth ships on Linux wrap in agent-jail, so a Linux lap asks a witness to launch the jail it is already inside. Nothing about the jail is broken; the guard reported the instrument it could not reach as the thing that failed.
think Verified every claim in that row before writing it rather than after -- the rish wrapper, the failing leg, the host and tier fields, the loop's own comment -- because %438 is one day old and its lesson is that my own finding reads as something I already know, so nothing in the sentence asks to be checked. What I could NOT measure from inside this sandbox is written into the row as not claimed: whether a host shell here, or any peer ship, reds the same way.
think Grade what you touch pushed one frame. amphora/README.md read C/74 at Door -- and grading the pre-edit file under a stash proved I found the reading rather than caused it, identical to the byte. The cause was 34 words of scoreable prose carrying 3 links, 9 per 100 against Door's 1.
think Refused the cheap closes. Reading a module front door at meter to buy an A would be exactly the being-too-smart Gauge forbids, and adding filler to move a denominator is worse. The honest repair was the one the page actually needed: a first-day reader learned nothing about what a vessel IS. Wrote the opening a Door owes -- one file, three verbs, and why seal-then-stamp is the whole design. Reach 20 to 90.
think That surfaced my own register at 33% negative against Door's 20%, so a Radiant pass rewrote the refusal sentences to lead with what the walls do, holding every fact. 8%. C/74 to B+/89. Then caught one claim of my own that overstated -- eight witnesses do not all plant; four do -- and made it exact.
think The hot pass earned its keep and caught me. seed_link was GREEN in the cold pass and RED in the hot one: other_living_links_outside_seed rose 848 to 849, one over a ratchet that only falls, because the roster link I added to amphora/README.md points into construction/ -- which template-manifest.bron line 272 marks `personal`, a room the seed withholds. The other eight links land in tools/, which the seed carries, and the arithmetic said so before I looked: nine links added, the ratchet rose by exactly one.
think Fixed it the way the scan itself instructs -- name it in prose instead -- so the page reads whole in both repositories. 848, verdict ok, seed_link GREEN, and the door holds B+/89.
obs This is %174's lesson landing on me in miniature: the cold pass measured a tree my commit was not going to ship. A round that stopped at the cold green would have shipped a broken front door into the public seed, and no cold pass would ever have said so.
obs The seat prompt said sixteen witnesses; the measurement says the eight crypto_kumara ones were already heard through crypto_suite, so the real silence was eight. A number handed to you is still a number to check.
obs A ratchet with 14 of slack has quietly stopped being a ratchet. Taking that slack is a one-line act and it is not a side effect of somebody else's round -- which is why it is named on the card rather than absorbed here.
obs The row was renumbered twice by the same law. Booked %439 from --next; a fetch found a peer had published %439 at 20260905.224714, earlier than my 20260905.230021, so mine moved to %440. A second fetch found ANOTHER peer had published %440 at 20260905.233435 -- LATER than mine -- and it moved again, to %446. The stamp orders the row and the anointed remote allocates the number, so a published number never moves and an unshared one yields even when its stamp is earlier. Twice in one send, which is the collision rate three writers actually produce.
obs A THIRD renumber, and a third fetch. The row went %439 -> %440 -> %446 across three sends' worth of peer landings in one hour, and the card crossed its 32,768 ceiling three separate times while I worked. Three writers at this pace is not a fault; it is the condition, and the pins' fold rules are what make it survivable. Rows %439-%441 -- two CLOSED, one BOOKED, all permitted by the pin's own key -- folded whole to one shelf, taking the ledger 29,856 -> 20,581, and four card accounts whose rows now stand on shelves condensed to pointers keeping every lesson sentence.
obs Twice tonight I typed a stamp into a shelf header or a cairn BEFORE the tool that would produce it ran, and both times the real clock disagreed. Caught both by reading the tool's own output rather than trusting the sentence I had just written -- which is exactly the %438 lesson I folded to a shelf in the same round.
obs A peer folded REDS %438 to a shelf eleven minutes before I folded the same row to a differently-named one, and theirs published first. The ledger's FOLD has no allocator the way its numbering now does -- same shape as %291, one room over. My duplicate shelf was withdrawn on the rebase and the card and the cairn repoint at the shelf that stands. The derived-spine law caught exactly what it was seated for, and the cost was one sed across three files rather than two rebases by hand.
obs The roster witness stays RED and honestly so: runs_unrostered=0 and every path resolves, and it refuses only because the run card records two reds. Neither is closable from this seat -- %431 sits at custody gate %1, and %446 is bakery's lane.
